course: dictionary
lesson: 4936
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Import is one of them, so you need the words around it to know which meaning the writer wants. Look at the clues first.
explain: «Exports are goods a country sells abroad and imports are goods it buys from abroad.» Here import is a noun, and the clue is abroad. Imports are things bought from another country, the opposite of exports.
explain: «Some people prefer local produce, like British apples at £2 a bag instead of imported ones at £1.50.» Imported ones are brought in from another country. Try your meaning in the sentence: does «brought in from another country» still make sense? Yes, so you have chosen well.
fact: Import means to bring goods into a country from another country to sell.
fact: An import is a product brought in from another country. Imports is the plural.
fact: Exports are goods sold abroad. Imports are goods bought from abroad. Trade is buying and selling between countries.
fact: In computing, to import means to bring a file or data into a program, like putting photos into a document.
fact: Import can also mean importance, as in «a matter of great import». This is a formal use, so it is good to recognise it.
exercise: «Exports are goods a country sells abroad and imports are goods it buys from abroad.» In this sentence, what are imports? | Goods that are lost at sea | Money a country keeps at home | *Goods a country buys from abroad | Goods a country sells abroad
exercise: Mia wants to import her holiday photos into her class document. What does import mean here? | *To bring a file into a program | To make a photo smaller | To sell goods to another country | To take a blurry photo away
exercise: «Some people prefer local produce, like British apples at £2 a bag instead of imported ones at £1.50.» What does imported mean here? | Grown on a farm nearby | *Brought in from another country | Given away for free
test: «A matter of great import» uses import in a formal way. What does import mean here? | Goods bought from abroad | Copying a file | Arriving late | *Importance
test: «A shortage is not having enough of something; countries that depend on imports are hit hard when trade is cut off.» What is a shortage? | A large bill for imports | Trade between two countries | Having more than enough of something | *Not having enough of something
exam: A country buys bananas from abroad to sell in its shops. Which meaning of import fits best here? | A product brought in from another country | Importance | *To bring goods into a country from another country to sell | Bringing a file into a program
